Host-volume GetVolInfo compatibility
===================================

check-files.ps1 volume-info probes the staged Windows ROMAW under GDB.
It compares root valence with PBGetCatInfo, checks that modification and
backup dates occupy their separate HFS fields, and verifies that a working-directory GetVolInfo returns that directory's valence while preserving the
WD reference. HGetVolInfo resolves the reference to the volume.

An isolated mounted folder checks external additions/removals, sidecar
filtering and modification-date refresh without opening Finder Get Info.
The fixture does not modify the contents of existing mounted volumes.

The calling and field semantics were checked against the user's reference:
Y:/Source Code/supermario/base/SuperMarioProj.1994-02-09/OS/HFS/TFSVOL.a,
GetVolInfo/RetVolInfo/retVRef and the working-directory valence path.

These checks cover root/working-directory metadata, not a recursive count
of all files on a host volume or complete Finder interaction.

GetVolParms is checked against OS/HFS/TFS.a and Interfaces/CIncludes/Files.h:
the complete version-2 record includes the unrated speed field and advertises
no unimplemented foreign privilege model. Requests return a contiguous prefix,
including odd lengths. Guards cover zero/negative/large lengths and null buffers.
The bNoDeskItems flag is clear for sidecar-capable host volumes and set for DOS
volumes. Toolbox/AliasMgr/FolderMgr.a checks it before looking up Desktop Folder. Finder was also checked displaying an
existing application from the ROMAW volume's Desktop Folder.

Host drive-root Finder information is stored below the user's
.romaw/volume-metadata directory. Root names are normalized and encoded;
long names use a fixed-size key with an exact volume.path identity check.
root.appledouble is the active record. Valid legacy %%2F records and their
._%%2F companions are imported into legacy-root.appledouble and legacy-sidecar.appledouble
before removal from the drive. Existing active records take priority;
conflicting, unrelated or malformed originals are retained. Ordinary file
and directory resource forks keep their existing sidecar locations.

check-files.sh root-metadata checks migration, complete fork preservation,
backup conflicts, identity checks, malformed input and profile failures on
Linux with ASan/UBSan and on Windows. It also checks catalog filtering of
legacy root records under netatalk conventions. The staged volume-info
probe writes, reads and restores a C-drive icon position and verifies the
actual FinderInfo bytes in the per-user record.
